PROMPT 219 — The Alvarez & Marsal CRO Assessment & Financial Restructuring
Act as Managing Director at Alvarez & Marsal's Financial Restructuring group.
Conduct a CRO (Chief Restructuring Officer) rapid assessment of a distressed company.
Deliver: (1) CRO appointment: when boards bring in an independent CRO vs. work
with existing CFO; CRO authority and reporting structure; typical engagement term.
(2) 13-week cash flow model: week-by-week cash receipts, disbursements, and ending
cash balance; minimum liquidity requirement; weeks until cash runs out without action.
(3) Immediate stabilization actions (0-30 days): (a) Critical vendor payments
(identify suppliers whose stoppage = business shutdown); (b) Payroll certainty;
(c) Customer communication to prevent accelerated receivables collection;
(d) Forbearance from lenders; (e) Discretionary spend freeze.
(4) Stakeholder assessment: secured lenders (cooperation vs. enforcement risk);
unsecured creditors (trade vs. bondholders); equity (retained leverage vs. dilution);
employees (retention risk); customers (contract termination risk).
(5) Strategic options: (a) In-court Chapter 11 (US) / Administration (UK);
(b) Out-of-court restructuring (faster, cheaper, less disclosure);
(c) Accelerated M&A (363 sale). (6) DIP financing: stalking horse process;
roll-up of pre-petition debt; priming lien issues; milestones.
(7) Reorganization value: enterprise value at confirmation; recoveries by class.
Format: CRO Assessment with 13-Week Cash Flow, Immediate Actions, Stakeholder Map,
Strategic Options Matrix, and Reorganization Value Analysis.
Inputs: [COMPANY NAME | INDUSTRY | WEEKLY CASH BURN | CASH ON HAND | DEBT STRUCTURE |
NEAREST MATURITY OR COVENANT BREACH | PRIMARY STAKEHOLDER CONCERN]

PROMPT 227 — The Cambridge Associates Endowment & Foundation Investment Policy
Act as Senior Consultant at Cambridge Associates. Design an Investment Policy
Statement (IPS) for a university endowment or private foundation.
Deliver: (1) IPS purpose and governance: who approves the IPS (Investment Committee);
review frequency; delegation of authority to staff and outsourced CIO (OCIO).
(2) Investment objectives: total return target (spending rate + inflation + fees);
real return target; how to set realistic long-term return assumptions.
(3) Risk tolerance: maximum acceptable drawdown; volatility tolerance; liquidity
requirements (what % must be liquid in 30/90/180 days).
(4) Asset allocation policy: target weights and rebalancing bands for each asset class;
how to set targets — MVO (Mean-Variance Optimization) adjusted for estimation error;
Black-Litterman model application.
(5) Alternative investments policy: PE, venture, hedge fund allocation limits;
vintage year diversification targets; minimum commitment size to maintain relationships.
(6) ESG policy: values-based exclusions required by board; ESG integration approach;
engagement and proxy voting policy; impact investment sub-portfolio.
(7) Performance evaluation: benchmark selection (custom policy benchmark weighted by
asset class weights; Cambridge PE and VC benchmarks); evaluation period (3, 5, 10 years);
manager review triggers.
(8) Spending policy: total return spending formula; smoothing rule (3-year average);
how to handle spending in a severe market downturn.
Format: IPS Template with Investment Objectives, Asset Allocation Policy, Alternative
Investments Guidelines, ESG Policy, and Performance Evaluation Framework.
Inputs: [INSTITUTION TYPE | AUM | ANNUAL SPENDING RATE | SPENDING AMOUNT |
INVESTMENT COMMITTEE COMPOSITION | ESG REQUIREMENTS | LIQUIDITY NEEDS]

PROMPT 334 — The Blackstone Real Estate: Hotel & Hospitality Investment
Act as MD at Blackstone Real Estate. Build an investment thesis for hotel
and hospitality real estate.
Deliver: (1) Hotel as an investment: shortest lease of any real estate (1-night);
operating leverage (high fixed costs → RevPAR changes drive outsized NOI impact);
brand vs. independent; management contract vs. franchise vs. owner-operator.
(2) RevPAR analysis: occupancy × ADR = RevPAR; RevPAR index (market share vs.
competitive set — penetration index); flow-through (% of RevPAR growth → NOI growth).
(3) Hotel segments: luxury (high ADR, lower occupancy, strong brand pricing power);
select-service (limited amenities, high margins, lower capex); extended stay (high
occupancy from longer stays, essential traveler demand).
(4) Post-COVID demand drivers: leisure travel recovery (outperformed pre-COVID);
business travel (BT recovery lag, permanent hybrid work impact on booking patterns);
group/meetings recovery.
(5) Brand franchise economics: Marriott/Hilton/IHG franchise fee (5-7% of gross revenue);
brand standards capex requirements (PIPs — Property Improvement Plans); brand switching.
(6) Value creation: (a) Management improvement (new operator contract, efficiency);
(b) Renovation (complete product upgrade → ADR and occupancy gain);
(c) Repositioning (change brand flag, convert use); (d) Ground lease optimization.
(7) Cap rate / NOI yield: hotel cap rates 7-9% (higher than office/industrial due to
operational risk); exit cap rate assumption critical in underwriting.
Format: Hotel Investment Framework with RevPAR Analysis, Segment Selection, Demand
Drivers, Brand Economics, Value Creation, and Cap Rate Analysis.
Inputs: [HOTEL TYPE | GEOGRAPHY | ROOMS | CURRENT REVPAR | ADR | OCCUPANCY |
BRAND/MANAGEMENT | ACQUISITION PRICE | RENOVATION BUDGET | TARGET IRR]

PROMPT 495 — The KKR Emerging Markets Private Credit
Act as MD at KKR's Emerging Markets Credit team. Build a private credit
investment strategy for emerging market borrowers.
Deliver: (1) EM private credit opportunity: bank retrenchment in EM (Basel III;
higher risk weights for EM borrowers); thin high-yield bond markets in most EMs;
direct lending to mid-size EM companies (equivalent of US/EU middle market).
(2) Target markets: India (NBFC-focused direct lending, RBI regulatory framework);
Southeast Asia (Indonesia, Vietnam — manufacturing growth, financing gap);
Latin America (Brazil — high rates create natural yield floors; Mexico — nearshoring).
(3) Credit underwriting in EM: same fundamentals (FCF, leverage, coverage) with EM
overlay (currency risk, political risk, governance risk); FX-denominated loans
vs. local currency loans — each has different risk profile.
(4) Deal structure for EM: hard currency (USD/EUR) loans to reduce FX risk;
local currency lending at high nominal yield; multi-currency structures with
natural hedging provisions.
(5) Governance and documentation: EM borrowers may have weaker legal documentation;
enforceability of security in EM jurisdictions; political risk insurance (MIGA);
offshore holdco structures for ring-fencing.
(6) Pricing: EM first lien direct lending 10-15% USD yield; mezzanine 14-18%;
reflects EM risk premium above US/EU equivalent.
(7) Portfolio construction: diversify across geography (no more than 30% one country),
sector, and currency; concentration limits; liquidity management for EM.
Format: EM Private Credit Strategy with Market Analysis, Credit Underwriting, Structuring,
Governance, Pricing, and Portfolio Construction.
Inputs: [TARGET MARKETS | CURRENCY APPROACH | CREDIT QUALITY | DEAL SIZE |
YIELD TARGET | SECTOR FOCUS | GOVERNANCE REQUIREMENTS | FUND SIZE]
